# list.bot -- the "list" namespace's ordinary Botlish members.
#
# The namespace's intrinsics are root natives registered under their
# qualified names -- list::length, list::at, list::append (core/lists.tcl)
# -- and this module may never define a member of the same name
# (surface/modules.tcl, DUPLICATE-NATIVE; STDLIB-NAMESPACES.md). Everything
# below is ordinary Botlish over them:
#
#   list::get(xs, i, default)   the element at i, or DEFAULT when i does
#                                not designate an element: exactly
#                                list::at(xs, i) with its IndexNotFound --
#                                and nothing else -- handled by returning
#                                DEFAULT. DEFAULT is an ordinary argument,
#                                evaluated before the call like any other
#                                (no lazy default); the result is never an
#                                optional/null value. Every other failure of
#                                list::at (a non-List or non-Int argument)
#                                propagates unchanged.
#
# and the search/reduction idioms built directly on the returning iterable
# loop (`loop x in xs:`, RETURNING-ITERABLE-LOOPS.md), Botlish's fundamental
# collection-transformation primitive.
# Deliberately no map/filter/filter_map: the returning loop already
# subsumes them (`loop x in xs: if not predicate(x): continue \n x` is the
# filtering idiom; the search/reduction helpers below are exactly the case
# where a genuine callback still pays rent).
#
#   list::any?(xs, predicate)   true iff some element satisfies predicate,
#                                left to right, short-circuiting at the
#                                first true. any?([], predicate) -> false.
#   list::all?(xs, predicate)   true iff every element satisfies predicate,
#                                left to right, short-circuiting at the
#                                first false. all?([], predicate) -> true.
#   list::none?(xs, predicate)  true iff no element satisfies predicate,
#                                left to right, short-circuiting at the
#                                first true. none?([], predicate) -> true.
#                                Written directly (not merely "not any?(...)
#                                "): a genuinely independent higher-order
#                                idiom, and another honest predicate call
#                                site for the next milestone's callvalue
#                                corpus -- not special-cased against any?.
#   list::find(xs, predicate)   the first element satisfying predicate,
#                                left to right, short-circuiting at the
#                                first match. No match (including an empty
#                                xs) fails the checked NotFound error below
#                                -- never null, undefined, or a sentinel.
#
# Each function's own returning-loop result is deliberately unused: every
# body short-circuits via `return` (or, for find, `fail`) before the loop
# would ever exhaust normally with a meaningful collected List. The native
# backend's discarded-result lowering (RETURNING-ITERABLE-LOOPS.md items
# 20-22, 28-29) applies at every one of these call sites, so none of these
# four helpers causes an output-List allocation the caller never asked
# for -- see that report's allocation census.
#
# No `continue` appears below, deliberately: it would only ever change what
# a *discarded* iteration contributes to a List none of these four
# functions ever reads (a skipped iteration's own `if`, with no `else` and
# nothing after it, already completes normally with `unit` -- ordinary
# Botlish `if`-without-`else` typing, ASCII-AND-URI-UNRESERVED.md's own
# `if not label_char?(...): return false` shape among many others -- so
# omitting `continue` here costs nothing observable and keeps the shape of
# each function to its one real decision). A `continue` remains load-
# bearing wherever a returning loop's own collected List *is* read -- see
# item 4's own filtering idiom, `loop x in xs: if not predicate(x):
# continue \n x`, in RETURNING-ITERABLE-LOOPS.md.
#
# `predicate(x)` is an ordinary dynamic call through an untyped parameter
# (`callvalue` at the interpreter/native level, exactly like lib/web.bot's
# own `scan_while`'s `predicate(char_at(i))`): this milestone intentionally
# does not add finite callable-target provenance, devirtualization, or any
# other optimization of that call -- see the report's higher-order
# callvalue census, and RETURNING-ITERABLE-LOOPS.md items 47/81.
namespace list

error NotFound

fn get(xs, index, default):
    value = list::at(xs, index):
        on IndexNotFound:
            default
    value

fn any?(xs, predicate) -> bool:
    loop x in xs:
        if predicate(x):
            return true
    false

fn all?(xs, predicate) -> bool:
    loop x in xs:
        if not predicate(x):
            return false
    true

fn none?(xs, predicate) -> bool:
    loop x in xs:
        if predicate(x):
            return false
    true

fn find(xs, predicate) errors NotFound:
    loop x in xs:
        if predicate(x):
            return x
    fail NotFound
